Logic:

I solved this problem using **backtracking** because, from every cell, I have multiple choices to explore (up, down, left, and right).

First, I iterate through every cell in the board because the given word can start from any position. Whenever a cell matches the first character of the word, I start a DFS from that cell.

In the recursive function, I first check whether the current cell is valid. If it's out of bounds or its character doesn't match the current character of the word, I immediately return `false`.

If I've matched the last character of the word, I return `true` because the entire word has been found.

Otherwise, I **mark the current cell as visited** by temporarily replacing its character with `'-'`. This prevents revisiting the same cell in the current path.

Then I recursively explore all four possible directions: **up, down, left, and right**.

After exploring, I **restore the original character**. This is the backtracking (undo) step, so the same cell can be used again in different search paths.

If any one of the four recursive calls returns `true`, I return `true`; otherwise, after trying all possibilities, I return `false`.

So the overall pattern is:

**Choose the current cell → Mark it as visited → Explore all four directions → Restore the cell → Return the result.**


Code:

class Solution {
    public boolean exist(char[][] board, String word) {
        boolean f=false;
        for(int i=0;i<board.length;i++){
            for(int j=0;j<board[0].length;j++){
                f= f || check(board,i,j,word,0);
                if(f){
                    return true;
                }
            }
        }
        return false;
        
    }
    boolean check(char[][] board,int r,int c,String word,int i){
        if(r<0 || c<0 || r>=board.length || c>=board[0].length || word.charAt(i)!=board[r][c]){
            return false;
        }
        if(i==word.length()-1){
            return true;
        }
        char ch=board[r][c];
        board[r][c]='-';
        boolean f =check(board,r+1,c,word,i+1) || check(board,r-1,c,word,i+1) || check(board,r,c+1,word,i+1) || check(board,r,c-1,word,i+1);
        board[r][c]=ch;
        return f;
    }
}
